---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Commit vs Socket — Structural Risk vs Active Threat Detection"
  description="Socket detects malicious code after it's published. Commit flags structural exposure before any attack. They answer different questions — and you need both."
  canonical="https://getcommit.dev/compare/socket/"
>
  <!-- Nav -->
  <nav>
    <div class="container nav-inner">
      <a href="/" class="nav-brand">Commit</a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="/#mcp">MCP</a></li>
        <li><a href="/audit">Audit</a></li>
        <li><a href="/watchlist">Watchlist</a></li>
        <li><a href="/extension">Extension</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog">Writing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a></li>
        <li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/piiiico/proof-of-commitment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">
        <a href="/compare/" class="breadcrumb">&larr; All comparisons</a>
        <h1 class="hero-title">
          Socket catches code after it's bad.<br /><em>Commit catches what makes it a target.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-lead">
          Socket.dev uses static analysis to detect malicious patterns in published packages — network calls, obfuscation, permission escalation. It's excellent at finding active threats. It also scores maintenance, including maintainer count, and alerts when a new npm owner starts publishing. What it does not do is treat reach as risk: its own scoring docs give popular packages a smaller penalty for negative metrics. Commit does the opposite.
        </p>
        <div class="key-stat">
          <span class="stat-icon">↓</span>
          <span class="stat-text">
            In the event-stream attack (2018) the malicious code sat in the package for over two months before a developer noticed. The warning sign was a change of owner: the author handed publish rights to a stranger. Socket's <a href="https://socket.dev/alerts">New author and Unstable ownership alerts</a> cover that case today.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Head-to-head -->
  <section class="hth-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">
        <h2 class="section-heading">Different layers of defense</h2>
        <div class="hth-grid">
          <div class="hth-card socket-side">
            <div class="hth-tool-label socket-label">Socket</div>
            <p class="hth-tagline">Catches active threats</p>
            <div class="hth-hits">
              <p class="hth-group-label hits-label">Catches</p>
              <ul class="hth-list hits-list">
                <li>Malicious code patterns (network calls, obfuscation)</li>
                <li>Typosquatting detection</li>
                <li>Install scripts and permission escalation</li>
                <li>Known malware in published versions</li>
                <li>Dependency confusion attacks</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="hth-misses">
              <p class="hth-group-label misses-label">Misses</p>
              <ul class="hth-list misses-list">
                <li>Publisher count weighted by install base (<a href="https://docs.socket.dev/docs/package-scores">its docs</a> damp penalties for popular packages)</li>
                <li>Dormant publishers who still hold access</li>
                <li>Whether a package is a high-value target</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="hth-card commit-side">
            <div class="hth-tool-label commit-label">Commit</div>
            <p class="hth-tagline">Catches structural exposure</p>
            <div class="hth-hits">
              <p class="hth-group-label hits-label">Catches</p>
              <ul class="hth-list hits-list">
                <li>Publisher concentration (credential risk)</li>
                <li>Download volume vs maintainer ratio</li>
                <li>Release consistency and cadence</li>
                <li>Full transitive dependency tree</li>
                <li>OpenSSF Scorecard integration</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="hth-misses">
              <p class="hth-group-label misses-label">Misses</p>
              <ul class="hth-list misses-list">
                <li>Active malicious code detection</li>
                <li>Typosquatting detection</li>
                <li>Install-time behavior analysis</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="verdict">
          <strong>Use both in sequence.</strong> Socket is your intrusion detection system — it catches threats after they're deployed. Commit is your risk map — it shows which packages are worth attacking before anyone tries.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Case study -->
  <section class="case-study-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">
        <h2 class="section-heading">Case study: chalk and lodash</h2>
        <p class="case-intro">
          The current releases of both are clean code: no malicious patterns, no obfuscation, no suspicious network calls. Commit flags both as CRITICAL, not because of what the code does but because of who controls the publish token. chalk shows why that matters: on Sept 8, 2025 a phished maintainer account published a malicious chalk 5.6.1 (<a href="https://osv.dev/vulnerability/MAL-2025-46969">MAL-2025-46969</a>). npm pulled it. Figures below read from npm on Nov 5, 2026.
        </p>
        <div class="case-grid">
          <div class="case-pkg-col">
            <div class="pkg-header">
              <span class="pkg-mono">chalk</span>
              <span class="pkg-meta">631M downloads/week</span>
            </div>
            <div class="case-card case-socket">
              <div class="case-tool-label socket-label">Socket</div>
              <div class="case-verdict-row">
                <span class="case-icon-ok">&#10003;</span>
                <span class="case-ok-text">No malicious code detected</span>
              </div>
              <p class="case-note">No suspicious network calls, obfuscation, or install scripts.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="case-card case-commit">
              <div class="case-tool-label commit-label">Commit</div>
              <div class="case-score-row">
                <span class="case-score">78</span>
                <span class="case-verdict case-critical">CRITICAL</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="case-reasons">
                <li class="reason-danger"><strong>1 npm publisher</strong></li>
                <li>631M downloads/week</li>
                <li>13 years old</li>
                <li>No provenance attestation</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="case-pkg-col">
            <div class="pkg-header">
              <span class="pkg-mono">lodash</span>
              <span class="pkg-meta">204M downloads/week</span>
            </div>
            <div class="case-card case-socket">
              <div class="case-tool-label socket-label">Socket</div>
              <div class="case-verdict-row">
                <span class="case-icon-ok">&#10003;</span>
                <span class="case-ok-text">No malicious code detected</span>
              </div>
              <p class="case-note">Clean publish history, no suspicious patterns detected.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="case-card case-commit">
              <div class="case-tool-label commit-label">Commit</div>
              <div class="case-score-row">
                <span class="case-score">78</span>
                <span class="case-verdict case-critical">CRITICAL</span>
              </div>
              <ul class="case-reasons">
                <li class="reason-danger"><strong>1 npm publisher</strong></li>
                <li>204M downloads/week</li>
                <li>14 years old</li>
                <li>Minimal recent updates</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Why structure matters -->
  <section class="structure-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">
        <h2 class="section-heading">Why attackers go for structure first</h2>
        <p class="structure-text">
          Socket is excellent at catching what it's designed to catch: malicious code after it's published to a registry. But there's a window between publication and detection. Sophisticated attackers exploit that window.
        </p>
        <p class="structure-text">
          Before an attacker writes a single line of malicious code, they pick a target. High-value targets share a structural profile: concentrated publish authority, high download volume, minimal organizational backup. These signals are stable, public, and measurable — they don't change between releases.
        </p>
        <div class="structure-insight">
          <p>When you know which packages are structurally exposed, you can:</p>
          <ul class="structure-list">
            <li>Prioritize monitoring resources on the highest-risk packages</li>
            <li>Set up alerts the moment a structurally exposed package publishes a new version</li>
            <li>Evaluate alternatives before an attack happens, not after</li>
            <li>Communicate risk to stakeholders with concrete data</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Evidence -->
  <section class="evidence-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">
        <h2 class="section-heading">Six attacks in 2026. Socket caught them after publication. Commit scores the conditions, not the code.</h2>
        <div class="attack-list">
          <div class="attack-row">
            <div class="attack-date">Mar 30</div>
            <div class="attack-info">
              <strong class="attack-pkg">axios</strong>
              <span class="attack-method">Token theft.</span>
              <span class="attack-detail">Socket flagged malicious code after publication. Commit flags it CRITICAL (1 publisher, 119M downloads/week), the condition the attacker used &mdash; scored after the attack, not before.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="attack-row">
            <div class="attack-date">May 11</div>
            <div class="attack-info">
              <strong class="attack-pkg">TanStack</strong>
              <span class="attack-method">CI/CD hijacking.</span>
              <span class="attack-detail">Compromised GitHub Actions published malicious versions. Socket detected post-publication. Commit did not flag it: TanStack scored 91 on healthy publisher signals.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="attack-row">
            <div class="attack-date">May 22</div>
            <div class="attack-info">
              <strong class="attack-pkg">TrapDoor</strong>
              <span class="attack-method">21 packages.</span>
              <span class="attack-detail">Planted persistence hooks in IDE configs. Targeted AI coding assistants. Novel enough to evade initial Socket detection.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="attack-row">
            <div class="attack-date">Jun 1</div>
            <div class="attack-info">
              <strong class="attack-pkg">Red Hat / Miasma</strong>
              <span class="attack-method">32 packages via compromised GitHub account.</span>
              <span class="attack-detail">Valid SLSA provenance on every malicious version. Socket eventually flagged; structural signals were present throughout.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="attack-row">
            <div class="attack-date">Jun 3</div>
            <div class="attack-info">
              <strong class="attack-pkg">Phantom Gyp</strong>
              <span class="attack-method">57 packages using binding.gyp bypass.</span>
              <span class="attack-detail">Self-propagating worm in the Shai-Hulud lineage. Novel bypass technique delayed detection.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="attack-row">
            <div class="attack-date">Jun 4</div>
            <div class="attack-info">
              <strong class="attack-pkg">IronWorm</strong>
              <span class="attack-method">37 packages with eBPF rootkit.</span>
              <span class="attack-detail">First npm supply chain attack with kernel-level persistence. Post-publication detection only.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="evidence-punchline">
          Socket is the right tool for catching attacks in progress. Commit is the right tool for knowing which packages to watch most closely — before the attack starts.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- Live compare -->
  <section class="live-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial" style="max-width: var(--max-content); margin: 0 auto 2rem;">
        <h2 class="section-heading">See your structural exposure</h2>
        <p class="live-intro">Five widely-used packages. Socket: no malicious code detected on any. Commit: live behavioral scores.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="compare-header-row">
        <div class="compare-pkg-label">Package</div>
        <div class="compare-col-header">
          <span class="tool-badge socket-badge">Socket</span>
          <span class="tool-desc">Malicious code detection</span>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-col-header">
          <span class="tool-badge commit-badge">Commit</span>
          <span class="tool-desc">Structural risk score</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="loading-state" class="loading-state">
        <div class="loading-spinner"></div>
        <p>Loading live Commit data&hellip;</p>
      </div>
      <div id="error-state" class="error-state" hidden>
        <p id="error-msg">Failed to load data.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="compare-rows" class="compare-rows" hidden></div>

      <div class="legend">
        <span class="legend-item"><span class="risk-badge risk-critical">CRITICAL</span> single publisher + &gt;10M downloads/week</span>
        <span class="legend-sep">&middot;</span>
        <span class="legend-item"><span class="risk-badge risk-ok">OK</span> healthy structural signals</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="faq-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial">
        <h2 class="section-heading">Frequently asked</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-q">Should I use Commit instead of Socket?</h3>
            <p class="faq-a">No. They're complementary. Socket detects malicious code after it's published — that's irreplaceable. Commit measures structural exposure before any attack occurs — that's a different layer. If you only have budget for one: Socket catches active threats. If you have both: Socket covers the post-publication window; Commit tells you which packages to watch most closely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-q">Does Socket prevent supply chain attacks?</h3>
            <p class="faq-a">Socket catches attacks after malicious code is published. The gap is the time between publication and Socket flagging it — which ranges from minutes to days depending on how novel the technique is. That window is exploitable. Knowing which packages are high-value targets in advance lets you tighten response time and prioritize monitoring on the packages most likely to be hit.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-q">How does Commit know which packages are targets?</h3>
            <p class="faq-a">Publisher concentration at scale is the primary signal. An attacker choosing a target wants maximum blast radius per credential compromised. A package with 1 publisher and 100M weekly downloads is structurally more attractive than one with 10 publishers and 10M downloads. That calculus is public and measurable — Commit tracks it across the entire npm registry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-q">Is Commit free?</h3>
            <p class="faq-a">The CLI, web audit, and API are free. Paid plans from $15–$29/mo add batch scanning, continuous monitoring, Slack/webhook alerts, and GitHub Action integration. <a href="/pricing" class="faq-link">See pricing &rarr;</a></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="section-divider" />

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="cta-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="editorial" style="text-align: center;">
        <h2 class="cta-heading">See your structural exposure</h2>
        <p class="cta-text">Find out which packages in your dependencies are high-value targets before anyone attacks them.</p>
        <div class="cta-buttons">
          <a href="/audit" class="btn-primary">Run a free audit &rarr;</a>
          <a href="/get-started" class="btn-secondary">Get a free API key</a>
        </div>
        <div class="cli-note">
          <code>npx proof-of-commitment --file package.json</code>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <div class="container footer-inner">
      <span class="footer-brand">Commit</span>
      <span class="footer-copy">Behavioral trust for the software supply chain.</span>
      <nav class="footer-links">
        <a href="/audit">Audit</a>
        <a href="/watchlist">Watchlist</a>
        <a href="/blog">Writing</a>
        <a href="mailto:pico@amdal.dev">Contact</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <style>
    /* ── Layout ── */
    .container {
      max-width: 64rem;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 0 2rem;
    }

    .editorial {
      max-width: var(--max-content);
      margin: 0 auto;
    }

    /* ── Nav ── */
    nav {
      position: sticky;
      top: 0;
      z-index: 100;
      background: var(--paper);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);
    }

    .nav-inner {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
      padding: 1rem 2rem;
    }

    .nav-brand {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      color: var(--ink);
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .nav-links {
      list-style: none;
      display: flex;
      gap: 1.5rem;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .nav-links a {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .nav-links a:hover { color: var(--ink); }

    /* ── Hero ── */
    .hero {
      padding: 5rem 0 3.5rem;
      background: var(--paper);
    }

    .breadcrumb {
      display: inline-block;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      text-decoration: none;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }

    .breadcrumb:hover { color: var(--ink); }

    .hero-title {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);
      line-height: 1.15;
      color: var(--ink);
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }

    .hero-title em {
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--accent);
    }

    .hero-lead {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
      max-width: 46rem;
    }

    .key-stat {
      display: flex;
      gap: 1rem;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
      background: #fff8f5;
      border: 1px solid #fca5a5;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      border-radius: 6px;
      max-width: 46rem;
    }

    .stat-icon {
      font-size: 1.2rem;
      color: var(--accent);
      flex-shrink: 0;
    }

    .stat-text {
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
    }

    /* ── Divider ── */
    .section-divider {
      border: none;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border-light);
      margin: 0;
    }

    /* ── Section heading ── */
    .section-heading {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 1.8rem;
      font-weight: 400;
      color: var(--ink);
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    /* ── Head-to-head ── */
    .hth-section {
      padding: 4rem 0;
      background: var(--paper-dim);
    }

    .hth-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      .hth-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }

    .hth-card {
      padding: 1.5rem;
      border-radius: 8px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      background: var(--paper);
    }

    .commit-side {
      border-color: var(--accent);
      border-width: 2px;
    }

    .hth-tool-label {
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .socket-label { color: #5b21b6; }
    .commit-label { color: var(--accent); }

    .hth-tagline {
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--ink);
      margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
      padding-bottom: 1rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);
    }

    .hth-group-label {
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
      font-weight: 500;
    }

    .hits-label { color: #166534; }
    .misses-label { color: var(--ink-subtle); }

    .hth-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.4rem;
    }

    .hits-list li {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      padding-left: 1rem;
      position: relative;
    }

    .hits-list li::before {
      content: '✓';
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      color: #166534;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    .misses-list li {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--ink-subtle);
      padding-left: 1rem;
      position: relative;
    }

    .misses-list li::before {
      content: '✗';
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      color: var(--ink-subtle);
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    .verdict {
      padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
      background: var(--paper);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      line-height: 1.6;
    }

    .verdict strong { color: var(--ink); }

    /* ── Case study ── */
    .case-study-section {
      padding: 4rem 0;
      background: var(--paper);
    }

    .case-intro {
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      line-height: 1.7;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
      max-width: 46rem;
    }

    .case-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
      .case-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }

    .case-pkg-col {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 1rem;
    }

    .pkg-header {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.75rem;
      padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);
    }

    .pkg-mono {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--ink);
    }

    .pkg-meta {
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--ink-subtle);
    }

    .case-card {
      padding: 1.25rem;
      border-radius: 8px;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .case-socket {
      background: #faf5ff;
      border-color: #e9d5ff;
    }

    .case-commit {
      background: #fff5f5;
      border-color: #fca5a5;
    }

    .case-tool-label {
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .case-verdict-row {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .case-icon-ok {
      color: #166534;
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .case-ok-text {
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: #166534;
    }

    .case-note {
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      line-height: 1.4;
    }

    .case-score-row {
      display: flex;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 0.75rem;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .case-score {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: 2.5rem;
      font-weight: 400;
      color: var(--ink);
      line-height: 1;
    }

    .case-verdict {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      padding: 0.2rem 0.6rem;
      border-radius: 3px;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    }

    .case-critical {
      background: #fee2e2;
      color: #991b1b;
    }

    .case-reasons {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.4rem;
    }

    .case-reasons li {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      padding-left: 1rem;
      position: relative;
    }

    .case-reasons li::before {
      content: '·';
      position: absolute;
      left: 0.25rem;
      color: var(--ink-subtle);
    }

    .reason-danger {
      color: #991b1b !important;
    }

    /* ── Why structure matters ── */
    .structure-section {
      padding: 4rem 0;
      background: var(--paper-dim);
    }

    .structure-text {
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      line-height: 1.7;
      margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
      max-width: 46rem;
    }

    .structure-insight {
      padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
      background: var(--paper);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      max-width: 46rem;
    }

    .structure-insight p {
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    }

    .structure-list {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }

    .structure-list li {
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      color: var(--ink-muted);
      padding-left: 1.25rem;
